    }), "\n", _jsx(_components.p, {
      children: "Hackathon projects built around AI features - LLM inference, image generation, embedding pipelines - frequently share an architectural pattern that causes demos to stall or feel unresponsive: every user action that involves heavy computation is handled synchronously within the HTTP request/response cycle. The user submits a form, the server calls the OpenAI API, waits for the response (often 3-15 seconds), and then returns a result. In a demo with one person and a fast internet connection, this is survivable. In any other context, it is a serious problem."
    }), "\n", _jsx(_components.p, {
      children: "The architectural concept at stake is the distinction between synchronous request handling and asynchronous task processing. For operations that take more than a few hundred milliseconds, the standard pattern in well-designed systems is to accept the request immediately, enqueue the work, and deliver the result through a polling mechanism or a push notification. This decouples the user experience from the latency of the underlying computation, allows work to be retried if it fails, and prevents the HTTP server from exhausting its connection pool waiting for slow operations to complete. Libraries like Bull or BullMQ in Node.js, Celery in Python, or even a simple in-memory queue for hackathon purposes make this pattern accessible within a short build window."
